course: religion-ethics
lesson: 79
title: Promises, memory and keeping each other safe
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Before he died, Grandad asked Ellie to look after his garden, and every week she waters his tomatoes: keeping a promise to someone who has died is a way of honouring them and keeping their love alive, and families try to follow wishes like Sophie's nan's, to have her ashes scattered at the beach where she grew up.
explain: But honouring someone is not the same as being forced: if Lily no longer wants to learn piano, her mum's wish does not trap her; she can find her own ways to remember, because love is not about forcing.
explain: Memory can be shared and given away: Mia retells her grandad's funny stories, Taras's family plant sunflowers every summer for his babusya (Ukrainian for «granny»), who loved them, and Ethan's family give Grandad's clothes to a charity shop so his kindness goes on.
explain: You may be honest about someone who has died, «Grandad was grumpy», as long as you are kind; but mocking someone's dead mum is cruel, and the right move is to tell a teacher straight away.
explain: The most important rule in this lesson: if a friend says or writes that they wish they were dead, or that they don't want to be alive, tell an adult you trust straight away, even if the friend asked you to keep it secret; you can also call Childline on 0800 1111.
explain: Christians and people of every faith and none believe every life is precious, so telling is being a good friend, not a snitch.
fact: Keeping a promise to someone who has died is a way of honouring them.
fact: Families try hard to follow the wishes of someone who has died, such as where their ashes are scattered.
fact: You do not have to do everything a person who died wanted, especially if it makes you unhappy; you can honour them in your own way.
fact: If a friend says or writes that they wish they were dead, tell an adult you trust straight away.
fact: Do not keep it secret, even if your friend asks you to.
fact: Childline is a free helpline for children in the UK: 0800 1111.
fact: Telling an adult when a friend is in danger is being a good friend, not a snitch.
fact: Telling someone's stories keeps them alive in people's memories.
fact: Many families give a loved one's clothes to people who need them and keep a few special things.
fact: Ukrainian families remember their dead with prayers and customs; «babusya» is Ukrainian for «granny».
fact: Making fun of someone's dead parent is cruel; tell a teacher straight away.
fact: People say «don't speak ill of the dead», but it is okay to be honest about someone, as long as you are kind.
fact: Christians and people of all faiths and none believe every life is precious.
exercise: Ellie promised Grandad she would look after his garden. Why does keeping it matter? | It honours him and keeps his love alive | Grandad will be angry with her if she stops | The garden belongs to the church now | She will get a prize for good gardening
exercise: Sophie's nan wanted her ashes scattered at the beach where she grew up. Why does the family try to do it? | Following her wishes shows respect | It is cheaper than any funeral | The law says ashes must go to beaches | The beach is the only place allowed
exercise: Lily's mum wanted her to keep learning piano, but Lily doesn't want to. What is true? | She can honour her mum in her own way | She must play piano every day for ever | She has broken her mum's heart in heaven | She must never touch a piano again
exercise: Jack's friend writes, «I wish I was dead.» What should Jack do? | Tell an adult he trusts straight away | Keep it a secret, just as his friend asked him to, because he promised | Tell him to cheer up and forget it | Wait a week to see if it passes
exercise: The friend begs Jack not to tell anyone. What is right? | Tell an adult anyway; it's too serious to keep secret | Keep the secret, because promises come first | Tell only the other kids in class | Post it online so people can help
exercise: What is the Childline number in the UK? | 0800 1111 | 999 9999 | 0800 0000 | 111 0800
exercise: Holly sees a girl crying alone who says she doesn't want to be alive. What should Holly do? | Stay with her if she can and tell an adult straight away | Walk away and leave her in peace | Tell her to stop being dramatic | Promise to keep it secret from everyone
exercise: Is telling an adult about a friend in danger snitching? | No; it's being a good friend | Yes; snitching is always wrong | Yes, unless the friend agrees | No, but only if a teacher asks first
exercise: Mia now tells her grandad's funny stories to her friends. Why is that good? | It keeps his memory alive | It shows she wants to be famous | It means she has forgotten him | It is the only way to stop crying
exercise: Why did Ethan's family give Grandad's clothes to a charity shop? | So people who need them can use them | Because keeping them is against the law | Because the clothes were cursed | So they would forget Grandad faster
exercise: What does «babusya» mean in Ukrainian? | Granny | Sunflower | Garden | Summer
exercise: Why does Taras's family plant sunflowers every summer? | To remember his babusya, who loved them | Because a law in Ukraine says every family has to do it | To sell the seeds at the market | Because sunflowers bring the dead back
exercise: A boy makes fun of Holly's dead mum. What should happen? | Tell a teacher straight away | Holly should hit him back hard | Holly should laugh along with him | Ignore it and hope it stops
exercise: Oscar asks if it's okay to say Grandad was grumpy. What is true? | Be honest, but say it kindly | Never say anything true about the dead | Say only bad things, to be honest | It's okay to mock him because he's gone
exercise: What does «don't speak ill of the dead» mean? | Don't say unkind things about people who have died | Never say the name of someone who has died | Don't talk at all when you're in a graveyard | Don't speak when you are feeling ill
exercise: Why should a friend's wish to be dead never be kept secret? | Every life is precious, and an adult can help | Secrets are always fun to keep | Friends never mean what they say | It's none of your business
test: «My mate said he wishes he was dead, but he said don't tell no one, so I won't, yeah?» asks a boy in Birmingham. What's right? | Tell an adult you trust straight away | Keep the promise; it's his business | Tell him to stop being soft | Wait and see if he says it again next week, then decide
test: A girl in Ohio texts, «she said she doesn't wanna be alive anymore, idk what to do» (idk = I don't know). What should she do? | Tell an adult straight away; Childline is 0800 1111 in the UK | Leave her alone for a while to calm down on her own, then text her tomorrow | Post about it so people know | Tell her it's just a phase
test: «Grassing on a mate is the worst thing ever,» says a boy in Glasgow (grassing = telling on someone). In this case, what's true? | Telling an adult when a friend is in danger is being a good friend | He's right; you must never, ever tell on a mate, whatever happens to him, ever | Only tell if the mate says you can | Tell other kids but not adults
test: The vicar says, very formally: «Every human life is of inestimable worth; should a friend speak of wishing to die, you must inform a trusted adult forthwith.» What does he mean? | Every life matters; tell a trusted adult straight away | Life has a price that can be measured | Only vicars may be told such things | Wait for the right moment to tell
test: «Your mum's dead, lol, good,» a boy says to Holly. What should she do? | Tell a teacher straight away | Hit him, because he started it | Pretend she didn't hear it | Agree so he leaves her alone
test: What would happen if Lily forced herself to play piano every day hating it, «because Mum wanted it»? | She might be miserable; she can honour Mum another way | It would bring her mum back to life | Her mum would be happy in heaven only if she kept on playing every day | Nothing; feelings don't matter here
test: «Grandad was a right grumpy git, can I say that?» asks Oscar from Bristol. Best answer? | You can be honest, but say it kindly | Never say anything true about him at all | Yes, and say even worse things about him too | No, because the dead were all perfect people
test: In a TV show, a girl must do everything her dead father wanted or «his spirit will never rest». What's a healthier view? | You can honour someone in your own way | You must obey every single wish for ever | Spirits come back and haunt you if you disobey | The dead control everything that we do now
test: «Why give Grandad's coat to the charity shop? That's cold, bro,» says Ethan's cousin (cold = heartless). What's a better view? | It helps someone who needs it, spreading his kindness | It shows the family never really loved him at all, deep down, or they'd keep it | Charity shops are only for rubbish | Coats should be buried with the dead
test: «We plant sunflowers for babusya every summer, she was mad about them,» says Taras in Toronto. What is this? | A creative way of remembering her | A Ukrainian law about growing sunflowers | A clever way of selling seeds to neighbours | A sign that she is coming back one day
test: Myth to check: «A true friend always keeps every secret, whatever it is.» | False; if a friend is in danger, tell an adult | True; every secret must always be kept, whatever it is | True, unless it's about sweets | False, but only at school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the obligation of a child whose friend expresses a wish to die? | To inform a trusted adult without delay, notwithstanding any request for secrecy | To preserve the confidence in accordance with the friend's request | To await a further such statement before acting | To disregard the statement as being without significance
exam: Identify the telephone number of Childline in the United Kingdom. | 0800 1111 | 0800 0000 | 999 9999 | 111 0800
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the wishes of a deceased person. | Families endeavour to honour them, though a child need not be bound to every wish | Every wish must be obeyed under penalty of law | Wishes of the deceased carry no significance whatsoever | Wishes may be honoured only by members of the clergy
exam: Whereas a pupil mocks a classmate concerning the death of her mother, ascertain the conduct most consistent with the matters taught. | The matter ought to be reported to a teacher forthwith | The classmate ought to retaliate by force, so as to deter any repetition | The classmate ought to join in the mockery | The matter ought to be concealed from all adults
